text:
Q: How to immediately return a 200 OK response to webhooks in PHP before running heavy background tasks? A: <p>Short answer: use the async queue as the real fix. fastcgi_finish_request() can mask
the symptom but doesn't solve the underlying problem and has real footguns for
webhook receivers.</p>
<p>Why you're seeing 504s</p>
<p>Your endpoint holds the HTTP connection open for the entire duration of
syncToCrmPipeline(). Under load, PHP-FPM workers are the bottleneck: each inbound
webhook occupies a worker for as long as the CRM call takes. If CRM calls are slow or
your CRM itself throttles/slows down under bursts, workers back up, the pool
exhausts (pm.max_children), and new requests queue behind nginx/gateway until they
hit fastcgi_read_timeout → 504. This is a queueing problem, not just a &quot;respond
faster&quot; problem.</p>
<p>Why fastcgi_finish_request() is a partial answer</p>
<p>It lets you flush the response to the client and keep executing PHP afterward — but
only under PHP-FPM (not mod_php, not php -S), and:</p>
<ul>
<li>The worker is still occupied for the full duration of syncToCrmPipeline(). You've
hidden the latency from the sender, but you haven't freed capacity — under a real
spike you'll still exhaust pm.max_children and start timing out (or refusing) other
requests.</li>
<li>No durability. If the worker process dies, PHP-FPM restarts, or the server
redeploys mid-request, that CRM sync is just gone — no retry, no trace.</li>
<li>No backpressure/retry semantics. A failed CRM call has nowhere to go but a log
line.</li>
<li>ignore_user_abort(true) is required or the runtime may abort you when the client
disconnects, even though you already answered them.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>  &lt;?php
  ignore_user_abort(true);
  $payload = file_get_contents('php://input');
  $data = json_decode($payload, true);

  http_response_code(200);
header('Content-Length: ' . ob_get_length());
  echo json_encode([&quot;status&quot; =&gt; &quot;accepted&quot;]);

  if (function_exists('fastcgi_finish_request')) {
      fastcgi_finish_request();
  }

  // Client already got 200 here, but this worker is still busy:
  syncToCrmPipeline($data); // no retry if this throws or the box restarts
</code></pre>
<p>Fine as a stopgap this week. Not a scalable pattern for &quot;high-volume spikes.&quot;</p>
<p>The queue approach (recommended)</p>
<p>Decouple accepting the webhook from processing it. The HTTP handler does only fast,
cheap work — validate + enqueue — so each request holds a worker for milliseconds,
not however long the CRM takes.</p>
<pre><code>  &lt;?php
  // webhook_receiver.php — fast path, stays under FPM worker for milliseconds
  $payload = file_get_contents('php://input');
  $data = json_decode($payload, true);

  if (!$data) {
      http_response_code(400);
      exit;
  }

  // (Optional but recommended: verify webhook signature here before trusting payload)

  $redis = new Redis();
  $redis-&gt;connect('127.0.0.1', 6379);
  $redis-&gt;rPush('crm_lead_queue', json_encode([
      'payload'    =&gt; $data,
      'received_at' =&gt; time(),
  ]));

  http_response_code(200);
echo json_encode([&quot;status&quot; =&gt; &quot;queued&quot;]);
</code></pre>
<pre><code>  &lt;?php
  // crm_worker.php — long-running consumer, run via supervisor/systemd, N processes
  $redis = new Redis();
  $redis-&gt;connect('127.0.0.1', 6379);

  while (true) {
      $job = $redis-&gt;blPop(['crm_lead_queue'], 5);
      if (!$job) continue;

      [$queue, $raw] = $job;
      $item = json_decode($raw, true);

      try {
          syncToCrmPipeline($item['payload']);
      } catch (Throwable $e) {
          // push to a retry/dead-letter queue instead of losing it
          $redis-&gt;rPush('crm_lead_queue_dlq', $raw);
          error_log(&quot;CRM sync failed: &quot; . $e-&gt;getMessage());
      }
  }
</code></pre>
<p>Why this wins for your case specifically:</p>
<ul>
<li>Worker capacity is decoupled from CRM latency. A slow or throttling CRM no longer eats into your ability to accept new webhooks.</li>
<li>Retries and dead-lettering are possible (RabbitMQ gives you this natively with DLX/TTL; with Redis you roll your own as above).</li>
<li>You can scale consumers independently of your web tier — add worker processes
during a known spike without touching nginx/PHP-FPM tuning.</li>
<li>Idempotency: since webhook senders often retry on slow responses, dedupe on a lead
ID / idempotency key either at enqueue time or in the worker, so a 504 that the
sender retried doesn't create duplicate CRM records.</li>
</ul>
<p>RabbitMQ vs Redis lists: RabbitMQ gives you delivery acks, retry/backoff, and
dead-lettering out of the box — better fit if CRM sync failures need robust retry
policies. Redis lists are simpler to operate if you already run Redis and are fine
building minimal retry logic yourself.</p>
<p>One infra note: don't just bump fastcgi_read_timeout to make the 504s go away — that
keeps workers blocked longer per request and makes exhaustion worse under a real
spike, it just delays when you notice it.</p>
<p>Bottom line: your infra team is right — move syncToCrmPipeline() off the request path
entirely via a queue. Use fastcgi_finish_request() only as a quick patch if you need
relief today while you build the queue, not as the end state.</p>
